WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.040
New overnight a potential sign of life as
rescuers race to find the submersible that

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.370
vanished while diving on the "Titanic" shipwreck.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:12.730
Sonar picked up banging sounds in the search
area and those were repeated, those sounds,

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.910
every 30 minutes according to an internal
U.S. Government memo.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.320
This morning the coast guard says it's not
clear what the source of the sound was and

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.090
Navy experts are analyzing it.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:28.949
It is estimated the five passengers have about
a day left of breathable air at this point

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:31.300
if that's submersible is still intact.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:38.149
Here is what a former passenger onboard that
same missing sub said what it's like inside.

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:42.790
&gt;&gt; As you start to descend through the water
column it is an amazing journey to see the

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:48.500
light quickly disappear and within five, ten
minutes you are in pitch dark.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.500
Complete dark.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.500
And you have the lights from the sub, right.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:51.809
You have the lights from the
[8:02:10 AM]

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:56.750
You have the lights from the submersible you
can see outside and internally.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:02.410
But without those, your light is gone at any
depth below a couple hundred meters.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:08.410
So anyone would be in complete darkness if
they didn't have power still at this point.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.090
&gt;&gt; Take a look.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:15.020
New video from this morning of another rescue
vessel heading out to join the operations

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.820
after the U.S. Air force flew special equipment
into Newfoundland.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.040
Miguel Marquez joins us in St. John's, Newfoundland.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.700
Can you talk to us about what the coast guard
is doing this morning?

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.610
&gt;&gt; Reporter: It is all hands on deck.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.119
There was a coast guard ship from the Canadian
coast guard that just shipped out.

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:41.040
Now there is the horizon arctic that private
ship that left overnight.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.470
Both of them carrying gear.

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:49.399
Both of them heading out to the search area
now in the hope that they can rescue the individuals

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:54.469
who may be as far as two miles down and looking
for help.

00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:56.320
With regard to the sounds that
[8:03:11 AM]

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.110
With regard to the sounds that were heard,
this was over about a four-hour period.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.480
There was a poseidon, a sub hunter that dropped
buoys into the ocean.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:06.659
It heard banging.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.710
Every 30 minutes they heard a regular banging.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.970
And then four hours later they dropped more
buoys in, heard more banging.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:19.860
They sent remote operated vehicles to the
location where they thought that sound was

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.550
